        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-01T12:46:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 774,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 711,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 118854,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 55,
        "headingId": 711,
        "headingText": "<p>Bone marrow biopsy sections usually show hypercellularity with infiltration of immature cells, sometimes associated with a starry-sky pattern. Smears show myeloblasts and cells with various degrees of myelomonocytic differentiation. Blasts are medium-sized or large, with abundant basophilic cytoplasm that is often vacuolated. Nuclei have delicate, lace-like chromatin and one or more prominent nucleoli. Promonocytes are considered blast equivalents (see also <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/63/31\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Chronic myelomonocytic leukaemia</em>).</p>\r\n<p>Peripheral blood smears typically show an increase in monocytes, which are often more mature than those in the bone marrow. The monocytic component may be more evident in the peripheral blood than in the bone marrow.</p>\r\n\r\n<h5>Immunophenotype</h5>\r\n\r\n<p>By flow cytometry, blasts commonly express myeloid-associated antigens such as CD13, CD15, CD33, CD65, and/or CD117. Monocytic markers are also typically present, including CD4, CD11b, CD11c, CD14, CD36, CD64, and CD163. CD7 and CD56 expression is common.</p>\r\n\r\n<h5>Cytochemistry</h5>\r\n\r\n<p>Blasts are positive (&ge;&nbsp;3%) for myeloperoxidase. The monoblasts, promonocytes, and monocytes are typically positive for nonspecific esterase and may also have myeloperoxidase-positive granules.</p>\r\n\r\n<h5>Differential diagnosis</h5>\r\n\r\n<p>The differential diagnosis of AML with monocytic differentiation should primarily include microgranular acute promyelocytic leukaemia and AML with <em>NPM1</em> mutation.</p>\r\n&nbsp;",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-01T12:46:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 775,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 712,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 118855,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 55,
        "headingId": 712,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>See above.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-01T12:46:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 776,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 713,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 118856,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 55,
        "headingId": 713,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Approximately two&nbsp;thirds of cases have a normal karyotype. The remaining cases show either isolated trisomy&nbsp;8 or rare structural abnormalities {27285584}. Frequently mutated genes include <em>TET2</em>, <em>RUNX1</em>, and <em>ASXL1</em>; less commonly mutated genes include <em>DNMT3A</em>, <em>STAG2</em>, <em>NRAS</em>, <em>IDH2</em>, <em>SRSF2</em>, <em>U2AF1</em>, <em>SF3B1</em>, and <em>ZRSR2</em>. <em>FLT3</em> internal tandem duplication (ITD) and tyrosine kinase domain (TKD) mutations are present in 25% and 10% of cases, respectively {27285584}. It should be noted that some of the aforementioned mutations now qualify for myelodysplasia-related AML (see <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/63/47\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Acute myeloid leukaemia, myelodysplasia-related</em>).</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-01T12:46:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 779,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 716,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 118857,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 55,
        "headingId": 716,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> &ge;&nbsp;20% blasts and blast equivalents (promonocytes) in bone marrow and/or blood; positive for myeloid-associated markers (e.g. myeloperoxidase, CD13, CD33, CD117); maturing granulocytes constitute &ge;&nbsp;20% of bone marrow cells; monocyte-lineage cells constitute &ge;&nbsp;20% of bone marrow cells; not fulfilling criteria for AML types with defined genetic alterations; not fulfilling diagnostic criteria for myeloid neoplasm post cytotoxic therapy.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-01T12:46:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
